{"metric":"hiscock","source":[{"metric":"hiscock","system":"flat","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"50bd6cee40afae03"},{"metric":"hiscock","system":"ingoing","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"e25857a360e5434f"},{"metric":"hiscock","system":"outgoing","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"09b12ec419a8448d"}],"views":[{"id":"history","label":"Forming and evaporating","box":[-0.35,3.4916,-3.3916,1.6623],"settings":"$m_0 = 1$, the mass of the shell and the unit of every length.","input":"$m(v) = 0$ before $v = 0$, $m_0$ until $v_1 = 2\\,m_0$, and $m_0\\cos^2(\\pi(v - v_1)/12m_0)$ until $v_0 = 8\\,m_0$: a shell of light of mass $m_0$ makes the hole, and negative energy falling in evaporates it. The surface of pair creation is $R = 3m$. $m(u)$ is the mass of the ingoing chart on the surface of pair creation $R = 3m$, where $u = v - 18m$: it is $m_0$ until $u_1 = -16\\,m_0$ and zero from $u_0 = 8\\,m_0$, and the radiation between carries positive energy outward.","caption":["A black hole that forms from a shell of light and evaporates, each point in the diagram a 2-sphere. Every event keeps the advanced time $v_-$ its ingoing ray had at $\\mathscr{I}^-$, and its outgoing ray left the centre of the flat space inside the shell at the advanced time $w$. Before the shell arrives $p = \\arctan((w + m_0)/3m_0)$ and $q$ is the same function of $v_-$, which puts that centre on the straight line $X = 0$. A ray of the flat space left after the hole starts at the new centre, and its $p$ is the $q$ of the ingoing ray it starts as, moved down to be continuous across the last ray $u_0$, which puts the new centre on a second vertical line.","The event horizon is the outgoing ray that reaches $r = 0$ just as the mass runs out, the last point of the singularity, and the ray that goes on from that point is the Cauchy horizon: every event above it has the end of the singularity in its past. The apparent horizon stands on one ray while the hole is Schwarzschild's and is timelike while the mass falls, so it ends inside the event horizon's future, at the same point. The radiation crosses $\\mathscr{I}^+$ between the rays $u_1$ and $u_0$, crowded against the Cauchy horizon by the redshift of the light that left just outside the horizon."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[0.0,-3.1416],[0.0,0.6435],[0.0,0.6435],[0.0001,0.6434],[0.0004,0.6431],[0.0009,0.6426],[0.0016,0.6419],[0.0025,0.641],[0.0036,0.6399],[0.0049,0.6386],[0.0064,0.6371],[0.0081,0.6354],[0.01,0.6335],[0.0121,0.6314],[0.0144,0.6291],[0.0169,0.6267],[0.0196,0.624],[0.0226,0.6211],[0.0257,0.618],[0.029,0.6147],[0.0325,0.6113],[0.0362,0.6076],[0.0402,0.6038],[0.0443,0.5997],[0.0487,0.5955],[0.0532,0.5911],[0.058,0.5865],[0.0629,0.5817],[0.0681,0.5767],[0.0735,0.5715],[0.0791,0.5662],[0.0849,0.5607],[0.0909,0.555],[0.0971,0.5491],[0.1035,0.543],[0.1101,0.5368],[0.1169,0.5304],[0.1239,0.5239],[0.1311,0.5171],[0.1386,0.5102],[0.1462,0.5032],[0.154,0.496],[0.1621,0.4887],[0.1703,0.4812],[0.1787,0.4736],[0.1874,0.4658],[0.1962,0.4579],[0.2052,0.4499],[0.2144,0.4417],[0.2238,0.4334],[0.2333,0.4251],[0.2431,0.4166],[0.253,0.408],[0.2631,0.3993],[0.2734,0.3905],[0.2838,0.3817],[0.2944,0.3727],[0.3052,0.3637],[0.3161,0.3546],[0.3271,0.3455],[0.3383,0.3363],[0.3497,0.3271],[0.3612,0.3179],[0.3728,0.3086],[0.3845,0.2993],[0.3964,0.29],[0.4084,0.2807],[0.4205,0.2714],[0.4327,0.2621],[0.445,0.2529],[0.4574,0.2436],[0.4699,0.2344],[0.4824,0.2253],[0.495,0.2162],[0.5077,0.2072],[0.5205,0.1982],[0.5333,0.1893],[0.5462,0.1806],[0.5591,0.1719],[0.5721,0.1633],[0.5851,0.1548],[0.5981,0.1464],[0.6112,0.1382],[0.6242,0.13],[0.6373,0.1221],[0.6504,0.1142],[0.6635,0.1065],[0.6765,0.099],[0.6896,0.0916],[0.7026,0.0844],[0.7157,0.0773],[0.7287,0.0705],[0.7417,0.0638],[0.7546,0.0573],[0.7675,0.051],[0.7804,0.